81. What is a transaction?
"A transaction is a collection of SQL operations treated as a single unit. Either all changes are saved or none are saved."
START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE acc_id = 1;

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance + 1000
WHERE acc_id = 2;
COMMIT;

Output
Before:
acc_id	balance
1	      5000
2	      3000

After COMMIT:
acc_id	balance
 1      4000
 2     	4000

82. ACID Properties
ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. These properties ensure reliable transaction processing and maintain data integrit

Atomicity - guarantees that all SQL statements inside a transaction must execute successfully, or none of them do
Sending money on Venmo. The app must deduct money from you and add it to your friend. If the app crashes halfway through, it cancels the deduction so your money doesn't disappear into thin air.

Consistency - A rule ensuring a transaction can only change data in ways that follow all database rules, types, and constraints. It prevents corrupt data from being saved
Setting a Password length. If a database rule states passwords must be at least 8 characters, the system will reject and block any attempt to save a 5-character password.

Isolation - A rule ensuring that multiple transactions running at the exact same time do not bleed into or mess with each other. Each runs as if it is the only one.
Buying the last PS5 on Amazon. If two people click "Buy" at the exact same second, the system locks the item for the first person. 
The second person is forced to wait, preventing the system from selling one physical item to two people

Durability - A guarantee that once a transaction is successfully completed and saved, the data is permanently written to disk. It will not be lost, even if the server explodes or loses power a millisecond later.
Posting a video on YouTube. Once you see the "Upload Complete" message, the video is safely saved on Google’s hard drives. 
If the YouTube data center loses power immediately after, your video is still there when the servers turn back on.



83. COMMIT
A COMMIT in SQL is the command that permanently saves all data changes made during the current transaction to the database
START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 70000
WHERE emp_id = 1;
COMMIT;

Before:
emp_id	salary
   1	  50000

After:
emp_id	salary
   1	   70000


84. ROLLBACk
A COMMIT in SQL is the command that Erases all changes made since the transaction started.

START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 70000
WHERE emp_id = 1;
ROLLBACK;

Before:
emp_id	salary
   1	  50000

After:
emp_id	salary
   1	   50000


85. SAVEPOINT
SAVEPOINT creates a temporary checkpoint that allows partial rollback within a transaction
START TRANSACTION;

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 60000
WHERE emp_id = 1;

SAVEPOINT sp1;

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 80000
WHERE emp_id = 2;

ROLLBACK TO sp1;

COMMIT;

Output

Before:
emp_id	 salary
  1	    50000
  2	    55000

After:
emp_id	 salary
 1	     60000
 2	     55000


86. Auto-Commit
Auto-commit mode automatically saves every SQL statement without requiring COMMIT
UPDATE employees
